  "statement": "The most profitable path for any enterprise is to reject the false binary of 'velocity versus safety' and instead recognize AI-assisted development as a major liability risk. While Accelerationist correctly identifies the pressure for market relevance and Safetyist appropriately demands rigorous governance, both overlook that high-velocity AI adoption creates a 'terminal maintenance burden.' Scaling a product is not a matter of how fast you can generate code, but of how effectively you can debug, own, and iterate on it without losing institutional knowledge. If your engineering team cannot explain or maintain the AI-generated logic currently fueling your MVP, you are not building a product; you are simply outsourcing your future bankruptcy to a black box.\n\nConsider the industry precedent of 'technical debt' in early-stage cloud migrations, where companies prioritized rapid deployment over architectural coherence, only to face massive, multi-year refactoring costs that wiped out their initial ROI. When AI acts as a force multiplier for code generation, it accelerates the arrival of this debt, creating a 'technical house of cards' where the system becomes too complex for humans to troubleshoot. This is not a theoretical risk; it is a measurable operational drag. Every hour saved in initial development through AI-assisted coding is frequently paid back with three hours of debugging and reconciliation once the system hits scale. To mitigate this, leadership must implement a 'Risk-Utility' audit for all AI-generated modules, treating them as third-party dependencies that require full human-in-the-loop verification before merging into core production branches.\n\nThis strategy necessitates a shift in how we measure developer productivity and talent retention. If we treat developers as mere operators of AI tools rather than architects, we degrade the very human agency required to navigate complex system failures. The cost of this erosion is high: when the